/-
Johnson/Desc/Kinds/Law/PadsViewFull.lean: a written PadsView (Johnson/Desc/Kinds/PadsTest.lean), and
Lean's kernel's evaluation that the cheap public test on the pads accepts it; with negative tests by
the kernel. No Mathlib.

WHERE THE TERM COMES FROM. padsFull WAS WRITTEN BY A PROGRAM, NOT BY HAND. A Lean script
(not in this library, 95c48f23...) evaluated padsViewOf at the arguments of
padsAtZ Z
(Johnson/Desc/Kinds/HeadDoorAtZ.lean), Z computed from the three public files of the full-size
stored example, and printed one line a pair and one line an oracle message. The printout's sha256:
312cb4d9b9ad6449330d733c8c82dcdb7ed6644734a03b231bece3d10258f9ce.
The program wrote these lines out as a Lean term and checked that the sequence of its 2026 numbers
is the printout's.

What is proved.
  padsFull            the written view: 24 pairs, each with its array; 167 oracle messages.
  padsFull_yes        padsV padsFull = true. Lean's kernel evaluates it.
  padsFull_no_pad     a negative test: the first pair's array's first rectangle is moved so far down
          and right that at no oracle message's shape it misses the pad: rejected.
  padsFull_no_array   a negative test: the first pair's array is missing: rejected.
  padsFull_no_mems    a negative test: no oracle message: rejected.
  padsDoor_of_view    whoever shows padsViewOf ... = padsFull has padsDoor ... = true.

Not proved here. THAT padsFull IS THE VIEW OF ANY ARGUMENTS: that rests on Lean's evaluator and one
printout; it is padsDoor_of_view's premise. Nothing on soundness.

-/
